VERDICT -- probe
================
CAPPING THE FIRST TRY WINS prob_1 AND LOSES THE BAND.  STOPPED EARLY; THE SIGN WAS NOT IN DOUBT.

        B (0.05)     H (0.05 + PROBE=2)         delta
  P1      571,668    531,403 / 531,403         - 7.0%   deterministic, both draws
  P3    4,468,072  4,517,165 / 4,517,165       + 1.1%
  P27     692,860    766,645 /   625,047       +10.6% / -9.8%
  P16   3,022,891  4,014,357 / 4,155,999       +32.8% / +37.5%
  P7      749,573  1,109,926 / 1,193,854       +48.1% / +59.3%
  P33     933,974  1,254,886                   +34.4%

THE PRE-REGISTERED FAILURE MODE IS EXACTLY WHAT HAPPENED, and the smoke had already shown it: with
cheap probes the OPSTAT profile came back with grow holding 90.7% of the budget and beam at 0.0 s.
Selection is `max(gain[i]/spent[i])`, and whichever operator first escapes the floor books the whole
floor-to-solution jump -- 978,969,222 on prob_1, against a final objective near 500,000 and against
pref's 155,451.  The winner of that race outranks every other operator by four orders of magnitude
for the rest of the run.

So a cheaper probe does not redistribute budget toward what pays; it changes WHO WINS THE RACE, and
the winner then takes everything.  On prob_1 the new winner was better by 7%.  On prob_7 it was
worse by 59%.

WHAT THIS ESTABLISHES FOR THE NEXT ARM.  A 30-60% swing from a two-second change to the opening
slice is not a tuning result; it is evidence that operator selection is decided by one accounting
artefact.  Not crediting the floor escape is what makes gain/spent mean marginal payoff again --
pref returns 36,262/s and grow 268/s on prob_1, numbers that currently have no influence at all
because both are invisible beside 978,969,222.

OGC_PROBE stays in the file at default 0 (off, byte-identical).  It is the instrument that found
this and is one env away if the gain fix changes its sign.

RETRACTION -- probe.log IS VOID
===============================
OGC_PROBE WAS ALREADY TAKEN.  It is the repair passes' opening slice, a FRACTION of the budget
clamped to [0.005, 0.50], defaulting to 1/(2n):

    _pf = min(0.50, max(0.005, float(_pv)))
    _rep = (1.0 / (2.0 * len(ops))) if _pf is None else _pf
    slot = [budget * (0.20 if o[3] else _rep) for o in ops]

The knob added today read the same name.  So every cell in this file ran with OGC_PROBE=2 meaning
BOTH "cap the first try at 2 s" AND "open every repair operator at 50% of the budget" -- six times
the 1/12 default.  The 30-60% losses on prob_7, prob_16 and prob_33 cannot be attributed to the
first-try cap, and the conclusion drawn from them -- that cheap probes change who wins the
floor-escape race -- has no support from this data.

THE IDEA WAS ALSO NOT NEW.  The pre-existing note at the same site records a previous session
running OGC_OPSTAT on prob_1 at 240 s, finding bay and z1 taking 21.3% of the worker for nothing,
and adding exactly this smaller-first-look for repair passes.  It also records why SEARCH operators
are deliberately exempt: "a beam either finishes or returns nothing, so a probe-sized slice starves
it rather than pricing it."  That is the design decision today's arm was unknowingly reversing.

The knob is renamed OGC_FIRSTCAP.  Re-running is possible but the prior is now poor: the exemption
it fights is documented and reasoned, and gainfair.log's J arm -- the clean half, with no
OGC_PROBE set -- lost 3.39% on its own.

NOT SHIPPED, AND THE SUBMISSION IS UNAFFECTED.  OGC2026_capfix.zip was built at 13:31, before any
of this was written; its three OGC_PROBE matches are the pre-existing implementation.
